# Yunzii B75 Pro Max -- access to the configuration channel only.
#
# The keyboard exposes four HID interfaces. Interface 0 carries the keystrokes:
# a rule matching on VID/PID alone hands every process running as the logged-in
# user a live keylogger. Only interface 1 (usage page 0xFF60, usage 0x61) is
# needed, so only interface 1 is granted.
#
# Why `modalias` and not the obvious `ATTRS{idVendor}` + `ATTRS{bInterfaceNumber}`:
# every ATTRS/KERNELS/SUBSYSTEMS condition in a single udev rule must match the
# SAME parent device. `idVendor` and `idProduct` live on the USB *device*, while
# `bInterfaceNumber` lives on the USB *interface* one level down -- so combining
# them produces a rule that silently matches nothing at all. The interface's own
# `modalias` carries the vendor and product IDs, which puts both conditions on
# one parent:
#
#   usb:v28E9p31C8d0109dc00dsc00dp00ic03isc00ip00in01
#       ^^^^^^^^^^                                ^^^^
#       vendor+product                            interface number
#
# Verified with `udevadm test` on the real device, against all four of its
# interfaces: only interface 1 matches (GROUP plugdev, MODE 0660). Interfaces
# 0, 2 and 3 do not match.
#
# Two access paths on purpose. TAG+="uaccess" covers a normal graphical login;
# GROUP="plugdev" covers the case where uaccess does not apply. Add yourself:
#   sudo usermod -aG plugdev "$USER"   # then log out and back in
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{modalias}=="usb:v28E9p31C8*", ATTRS{bInterfaceNumber}=="01", MODE="0660", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"
